    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d68">

                <pb facs="0131" n="105" sameAs="frus:frus1955-57v05/pg105"/>

                <head>68. Telegram From the Department of State to the
                        Secretary of State, at Paris<anchor n="1"
                        xml:id="d68-f1"/></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1955-05-12T11:03:00-05:00"><hi rendition="#italic">May 12,
                                1955—11:03 a.m.</hi></date></dateline></opener>
                <p>Tedul 24. Eyes only Secretary from the
                    President. Thank you very much for keeping me so completely informed. Your
                    cables are intensely interesting.</p>
                <pb facs="0132" n="106" xml:id="pg106"/>
                <p>I fully approve your courses of action in regard to the Austrian Treaty, though I
                    am of course hopeful that the situation will so develop that it will be possible
                    for us to participate in the final signing of the document in Vienna with the
                    other powers over the coming weekend.<anchor n="2" xml:id="d68-f2"/> Warm
                    regard, DE.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#bold"
                            >Hoover</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d68-f1" n="1" type="source" xml:id="d68-f1-content">
                <p>Source: Department of State, Central Files, 663.001/5–1255. Secret; Niact.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d68-f2" n="2" xml:id="d68-f2-content">
                <p>At 9 p.m. on May 12 Secretary Dulles
                    replied:</p>
                <p>“I greatly appreciate your personal message. I think the situation has now
                    developed so that we can sign the Austrian Treaty on Sunday. The Soviets have
                    given way, and I am proceeding to Vienna tomorrow morning.” (Dulte 36 from Paris; <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >ibid.</hi>)</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
